    <h1>How it works</h1>
    <h2>1. You provide a topic or word list</h2>
    <p>Topic mode asks AI for candidates. Word list and manual modes keep your answers under your control.</p>
    <h2>2. AI drafts content — not the grid</h2>
    <p>The model may suggest titles, answers, and clues. It cannot write coordinates, numbering, validation flags, prices, or entitlements.</p>
    <h2>3. The program builds and checks the puzzle</h2>
    <p>A deterministic TypeScript engine places interlocking words with a seed and search budget, then an independent validator rescans the grid for ghost words and mismatches.</p>
    <h2>4. You review, edit, and export</h2>
    <p>Statuses mean different things: <strong>Grid checks passed</strong> is structural only; <strong>Clues need review</strong> means AI text is unchecked; <strong>Reviewed by you</strong> is your confirmation.</p>
    <h2>What we do not promise</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>That every word list packs perfectly</li>
      <li>That clues are factually perfect without review</li>
      <li>Newspaper-style symmetry or cryptic clues</li>
      <li>“AI verified” or “guaranteed unique solution” badges</li>
    </ul>
